    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"394485\">suhr</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/579630-Project-announcements/topic/Prismriver.3A.20Formalization.20of.20Music.20Theory/near/610841886\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><del>A question: does it support microtonality?</del> It seems like it does.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>It does but you would have to define you own pitch type for it.</p>",
        "id": 610891243,
        "sender_full_name": "Leni Aniva",
        "timestamp": 1784134598
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"599027\">Leni Aniva</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/579630-Project-announcements/topic/Prismriver.3A.20Formalization.20of.20Music.20Theory/near/610891243\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"394485\">suhr</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/579630-Project-announcements/topic/Prismriver.3A.20Formalization.20of.20Music.20Theory/near/610841886\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><del>A question: does it support microtonality?</del> It seems like it does.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>It does but you would have to define you own pitch type for it.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Prismriver's <a href=\"https://codeberg.org/aniva/Prismriver/src/branch/main/Prismriver/Repr/Scale.lean#L93\">Equal temperament tuning</a> uses an Int for Pitch. So the code would be similar to namespace EqualTemp, except define your own pitch type (instead of using Int) to adjust for microtonality right? You'll have to re-implement the typeclasses (Torsor P I, Scale P I) too.</p>",
        "id": 610900791,
        "sender_full_name": "Claire Wang",
        "timestamp": 1784137761
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"679328\">Claire Wang</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/579630-Project-announcements/topic/Prismriver.3A.20Formalization.20of.20Music.20Theory/near/610900791\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"599027\">Leni Aniva</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/579630-Project-announcements/topic/Prismriver.3A.20Formalization.20of.20Music.20Theory/near/610891243\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"394485\">suhr</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/579630-Project-announcements/topic/Prismriver.3A.20Formalization.20of.20Music.20Theory/near/610841886\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><del>A question: does it support microtonality?</del> It seems like it does.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>It does but you would have to define you own pitch type for it.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Prismriver's <a href=\"https://codeberg.org/aniva/Prismriver/src/branch/main/Prismriver/Repr/Scale.lean#L93\">Equal temperament tuning</a> uses an Int for Pitch. So the code would be similar to namespace EqualTemp, except define your own pitch type (instead of using Int) to adjust for microtonality right? You'll have to re-implement the typeclasses (Torsor P I, Scale P I) too.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Yes. I was going to make <code>Accidental</code> a rational or dyadic rational type, but decided against it since people may want other forms of microtonality.</p>",
        "id": 610901415,
        "sender_full_name": "Leni Aniva",
        "timestamp": 1784137957
    },
    {
